  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The game was decided in the first four innings. Arizona scored six runs before Gage Jump left after 4.2 innings, and the Athletics never got within striking distance. This was a blowout early, made official when the margin became insurmountable by the fifth.",
    "what_this_means": "Inconsistency isn't a neutral word anymore. It's a diagnosis. They built a one-run lead in the third, then watched it evaporate in the fourth when Arizona struck for two, and again in the fifth when the Diamondbacks scored seven. Ten hits and five strikeouts suggest the lineup was at least present, and McNeil's solo homer gave them a moment of their own, but moments don't matter when the pitching breaks that early. Yunior Tur gave up eight runs in two-thirds of an inning. The game was not close after that. When the starter exits before the fifth and the bullpen gets punished immediately after, there's no comeback math that saves you. They're still leaving runners on base at alarming rates. 12 RISP situations, only four converted. But that only matters if the pitching can keep you in the game long enough to cash them. Jump couldn't hold the line. The margin disappeared before they had time to find it.",
    "what_to_watch": "Minnesota starts Zebby Matthews tomorrow, a pitcher with an ERA near five who has given up everything the Athletics could use right now.",
